-- **Meltable block** (`res://scripts/meltable_block.gd`, `class_name MeltableBlock`): base class of `IceBlock` and `MetalBlock`; a subclass supplies only what differs — `_apply_forces()` (gravity/buoyancy/drag into `velocity`), `_touching_slag()`, `_melt_liquid_type()`, `_melt_sound()`, `_shunt_sound()`, `_draw_block()` (and `is_slippery()` on ice). Don't override `_ready()`/`_physics_process()` without calling `super()`. Inspector values that differ per subclass live in the subclass's `.tscn` (metal_block.tscn overrides `melt_time`, `slag_melt_time`, `cool_rate`, `shunt_speed`, `float_depth_fraction`), not in `_init()`, because Godot skips saving a value equal to the base script's default. `@tool` `RigidBody2D` on layers 1|2, mask 1|2, sized in 16px tiles via `size_tiles` (its collider is an octagon, corners bevelled by `chamfer_px`, default 1, so it doesn't snag on tile seams; each scene holds a `ConvexPolygonShape2D` that is `resource_local_to_scene`, so resizing one instance never touches another), falling under gravity until it lands. Rotation is free by default; an instance sets `allow_rotation` false to lock it upright (see Rotation below). Gravity, buoyancy and drag are integrated by hand (`gravity_scale` 0, engine damping replaced with 0, no sleeping, a friction-0 physics material, contact reporting on — all set in `_configure_body()`), so the constants keep their meaning and the engine only solves contacts; `velocity` is a scratch copy valid inside `_physics_process()` and the subclass hooks, and the real velocity is `linear_velocity`. Horizontal motion is **own motion** (`_own_vx`: kick, its decay, liquid drag, underside push) plus **carry** (conveyor speed, and with `carry_assist` the velocity of the world body under it, see Moving floors below), written to `linear_velocity` each frame; `_integrate_forces()` runs first each tick, scans the previous step's contacts (floor, wall, actor side, belt, floor velocity — read orientation-independently) and accepts the solver's change to the written velocity into own motion. **Static friction** is `_is_held()` (the engine contact has friction 0): an idle block on a floor no steeper than `hold_slope_degrees` has gravity cancelled, so it neither creeps nor sinks; on a steeper floor gravity acts and it slides (ice 8° in `ice_block.tscn`, metal and the base default 30°). A rotating block is held only while its floor contacts sit under its centre of mass (give or take `SUPPORT_TOLERANCE`) and while it leans no more than `HOLD_TILT_MARGIN` beyond the surface under it, so one overhanging a ledge still topples. **Moving floors:** carry is the floor's velocity at the block's *centre* in both axes (the contact's velocity plus `ω × lever`, with ω from the floor body's `get_angular_velocity()` when it has one — `RotatingBody` and rigid blocks do, tiles report 0), and a held block takes the floor's vertical velocity instead of zero, so a tilting or lifting platform never presses into it (a frictionless, tilted push would otherwise leak into own motion and creep it off); `carry_assist = false` turns both off. On a body floor (a plank, another block) a held rotation-allowed block also takes the floor's angular velocity, discarding the solver's angular change, and its righting spring targets the floor's tilt (`_floor_tilt`, from the flattest floor contact; 0 for tiles) instead of world-upright, so it turns with a plank, including through a swing reversal. The contact report can drop one corner of a flat contact on a moving body for a frame or two, so the support test is latched for `SUPPORT_LATCH_FRAMES` (12) on body floors only; tile floors keep the strict test and a block still topples off a tile ledge at once. `debug_hold` prints floor/hold state about six times a second while the block rests on a floor. Each instance copies its collision shape in `_ready()` as a backstop. **Weight:** the engine's `mass` is the block's weight, set per 16px tile in the scene (metal 4, ice 1) and multiplied by the block's area in `_configure_body()`; it sets how contacts trade momentum between blocks and is what a rotating body reads as a block's load, and has no part in gravity or buoyancy (hand-integrated per unit mass). Joins `"water_solid"` and `"ground"` while intact. Implements `on_laser_hit()` by stamping `Engine.get_physics_frames()`; its own physics step treats a hit within the last 2 frames as lit, so it is independent of turret processing order. Heat: lit, `melt_progress` rises over `melt_time`; touching slag, over `slag_melt_time`; the faster wins; with neither it drains at `cool_rate`. At 1.0 it disables collision (deferred), leaves both groups, plays the subclass's melt sound, calls `WaterManager.spawn_melt_particles(cell, liquid_type, world_center, rotation)` once per 16px cell it covers, passing each cell's exact world position and the block's rotation so the burst is spread over the cell in the block's own frame (a snapped, axis-aligned spread started particles inside the plank under a block resting on a see-saw, where they fell through), and frees itself (the manager tolerates a freed registered solid). **Art tiling** (`_draw_art(texture, tile_origin, rect)`, with `mirror_tiling` exported, default on): a block bigger than one 16px tile along an axis doesn't repeat the tile per cell. The first and last 75% of the tile (source 0–12px at the start, 4–16px at the end) appear only at the block's two ends, and the span between is filled with the tile's middle band (source 4–12px, `MIRROR_EDGE_FRACTION` 0.25) ping-ponged, every copy mirrored against its neighbours, so a 2-tile axis is 0–0.75 normal, 0.75–1.25 the mirrored band, 1.25–2 normal. Each axis is independent, boundaries are whole pixels, and mirrored pieces are drawn through a flipped `draw_set_transform_matrix()` (reset afterwards). A 1-tile axis, or `mirror_tiling` off, draws the plain tile. Subclass sprites go through this helper. Kickable: `kicked(direction)` (the optional kick contract in player.gd, alongside `take_damage()`) sets its own horizontal motion to `shunt_speed` away from the kicker, decaying at `slide_friction` while on the floor; it can slide off ledges and stops against walls and other bodies; `shunt_speed <= 0` makes a block unkickable. **Surf shove** (`surf_shoved(direction, surf_speed) -> float`, the optional contract the player's bowl sweep reads): the block's own motion becomes `surf_speed * surf_shove_transfer / sqrt(mass)` (default transfer 0.5, so a heavy block takes less), never below `shunt_speed` and capped at `surf_shove_max_speed` (200; on ice that slides about 18 tiles at `slide_friction` 70), and a block already sliding that way is never slowed; the cost to the surfer is `given * mass / (mass + 2)` (2 mirrors the see-saw's `player_weight`). It returns -1 and refuses when `shunt_speed <= 0`, when melted, or when `_hit_wall` is set on the side the surfer pushes from (`_wall_side` from the last contact scan: a tile wall or another body pressed against that face), so such a block stops the surf like a wall; a block 1 px short of a wall is still shoved and the surfer then passes through it into the wall. Plays the block's shunt sound. Inspector: group "Surf". **Jump pad launch** (`launch(vector)`, the duck-typed contract `jump_pad.gd` calls on anything that has it, as on the player): the vector is divided by `mass ^ launch_weight_exponent` (default 1; mass is per tile times area, so a 1x1 ice block takes it whole, a 1x1 metal block a quarter of the speed, about half a tile of height from a 500 px/s pad, and a bigger block lifts less) and buffered in `_launch_vector`; `_physics_process()` applies it once, after the hook, floor friction and the held-block vertical zero have edited `velocity` and just before the write to `linear_velocity`, replacing the block's velocity outright (own motion and vertical), so nothing in the tick can cancel it and the pad's Area2D signal can fire at any point in the step. The pad triggers on `body_entered`, so a block is launched once per entry (a block that lands back on the pad is launched again); a melted block ignores it, an unkickable one is launched all the same, a hanging player is released by the block's speed (`hang_max_speed`), and the buffer isn't saved. The hand-integrated gravity clamps vertical speed to ±`MAX_FALL_SPEED` (400) on the tick after, so a straight-up launch is capped at 400 px/s whatever the pad's `launch_speed`. Inspector: group "Launch". **Shatter** (`shatter()`, the contract `crusher.gd` calls): if the subclass's `_shatters_when_crushed()` hook is true (ice; false in the base class and for metal) it runs `_melt()` at once, the block's own melt path rather than a separate delete, so it plays its melt sound, spawns the same per-tile water burst as a full melt, and frees itself; a block that doesn't shatter, or has already melted, ignores it. Buoyancy helpers: `_submerged_fraction(liquid_type)` (the area of the block's rotated rectangle below the settled liquid surface of the basin at its centre column, a polygon clip, via `WaterManager.get_basin_for_column()` and `get_liquid_surface_world_y()` so crust doesn't count; it also records the submerged area's centroid, the centre of buoyancy) and `_buoyant_accel(submerged, riders, liquid_type)`, `GRAVITY * (1 - submerged / rest_fraction)` (plus, with rotation allowed, the couple between the centre of buoyancy and the centre of mass as angular acceleration) where `rest_fraction = float_depth_fraction + riders * rider_load_tiles / block area` — zero net force at the rest fraction, upward when deeper, and `rest_fraction >= 1.0` can't carry the load and sinks (the carrying-capacity lever for level design). Riders: `_actors_in_band()` (a thin `intersect_shape` band across the top face of the block taken as an upright rectangle, width and height swapped on its side, counting layer-1-only bodies, never a player hanging from this block) is applied the instant a rider is detected, so behaviour doesn't depend on the block having settled. **Rising and draining levels:** a floating block rides the surface. `_damp_vertical_in_liquid(coefficient, submerged, riders, liquid_type, delta)` (used by ice and by metal on slag) damps `velocity.y` relative to the liquid's own vertical speed, `WaterBasin.get_liquid_surface_velocity_y()` scaled by `submerged / rest_fraction` (clamped 0..1), so a block at its rest fraction follows a rising or draining level instead of lagging it, and the surface can't overtake a rider's swim probe. A rider can't hold the block back: in `_integrate_forces()`, while an actor touches the top face (`_rider_on_top`), no roof is hit and every rider has room above (`_riders_free_above`, a `test_move()` per rider in the physics step), a solver-reduced rise (`vel.y > _written_vy` with `_written_vy < 0`) is reset to the written velocity, and the rider is pushed up by its own depenetration. Under a ceiling the contact stops the block as before. The player completes it from its side by leading a rigid floor (see Rising rigid floors under Player). Dropping layer 1 from the block's mask does not work on a rigid body (a pair interacts if either side's mask matches), and a collision exception against the rider makes it fall through (below). **Don't use a collision exception against the rider** — Godot applies exceptions to both bodies, so the rider would fall through. Ledge-hangable via group `"ground"` (`hang_platform` is typed `PhysicsBody2D`, not `AnimatableBody2D`, for this reason; the hang follows the block's whole transform as it bobs, slides or tilts, `_follow_hang_platform()` mapping every pinned point through the platform's change of transform; `is_hangable()` is the block's say: a locked block always, a rotation-allowed one only within `hang_max_tilt_degrees` (10) of resting flat, at most `hang_max_speed` (90 px/s) and `hang_max_spin` (1.2 rad/s), refusing the grab otherwise and force-releasing a held hang (`_force_release_hang()` in `player.gd`, which also stops a mantle in progress); a melt releases a hanging player by clearing `is_hanging`, as `drop_platform.gd` does). While a player's hang is attached to the block (grab through the end of the mantle), the block and player hold a mutual collision exception (`_update_hanger_exception()`), dropped the frame the hang ends: the mantle tween carries the player's collider through the block's top corner, and without the exception the contact between them pushes the block while `_follow_hang_platform()` drags the mantle target with it, so the push can feed itself. **Any other actor-driven teleport onto a `CharacterBody2D` platform needs the same treatment.** Carried by conveyors: `_scan_contacts()` reads `get_conveyor_speed()` off the floor it rests on (ice and metal alike, regardless of slipperiness or weight) as carry, added to the written velocity but never to own motion; a block carried into a wall stops there, with no stored push-back left to creep it away. **Rotation** (`allow_rotation`, default true; false = locked upright): hand-integrated in `_integrate_forces()` like the rest. The solver's angular change (a corner landing, a ledge overhang) is accepted, then a soft spring pulls toward the nearest 90° (`righting_stiffness`) and angular drag (`angular_drag`, plus `angular_drag_liquid` scaled by the submerged fraction) damps it, so a block topples when its support goes but settles flat, upright or on its side. The centre of mass is lowered by `com_drop` (a fraction of half the height). The buoyancy couple lifts a tilted floating block's low side. Riders apply their weight straight down at their feet: a torque `lever_x * weight / gyration²` about the centre of mass (`_rider_torque()`, with `rider_load_tiles` setting how hard), cancelled while the block and its riders together balance over the floor contacts (`RIDER_SUPPORT_TOLERANCE`), so a rider near the edge of a supported block doesn't rock it or the blocks stacked on it, while a rider on the overhang of a