ayer.gd, alongside `take_damage()`) sets its own horizontal motion to `shunt_speed` away from the kicker, decaying at `slide_friction` while on the floor; it can slide off ledges and stops against walls and other bodies; `shunt_speed <= 0` makes a block unkickable. **Surf shove** (`surf_shoved(direction, surf_speed) -> float`, the optional contract the player's bowl sweep reads): the block's own motion becomes `surf_speed * surf_shove_transfer / sqrt(mass)` (default transfer 0.5, so a heavy block takes less), never below `shunt_speed` and capped at `surf_shove_max_speed` (200; on ice that slides about 18 tiles at `slide_friction` 70), and a block already sliding that way is never slowed; the cost to the surfer is `given * mass / (mass + 2)` (2 mirrors the see-saw's `player_weight`). It returns -1 and refuses when `shunt_speed <= 0`, when melted, or when `_hit_wall` is set on the side the surfer pushes from (`_wall_side` from the last contact scan: a tile wall or another body pressed against that face), so such a block stops the surf like a wall; a block 1 px short of a wall is still shoved and the surfer then passes through it into the wall. Plays the block's shunt sound. Inspector: group "Surf". **Jump pad launch** (`launch(vector)`, the duck-typed contract `jump_pad.gd` calls on anything that has it, as on the player): the vector is divided by `mass ^ launch_weight_exponent` (default 1; mass is per tile times area, so a 1x1 ice block takes it whole, a 1x1 metal block a quarter of the speed, about half a tile of height from a 500 px/s pad, and a bigger block lifts less) and buffered in `_launch_vector`; `_physics_process()` applies it once, after the hook, floor friction and the held-block vertical zero have edited `velocity` and just before the write to `linear_velocity`, replacing the block's velocity outright (own motion and vertical), so nothing in the tick can cancel it and the pad's Area2D signal can fire at any point in the step. The pad triggers on `body_entered`, so a block is launched once per entry (a block that lands back on the pad is launched again); a melted block ignores it, an unkickable one is launched all the same, a hanging player is released by the block's speed (`hang_max_speed`), and the buffer isn't saved. The hand-integrated gravity clamps vertical speed to ±`MAX_FALL_SPEED` (400) on the tick after, so a straight-up launch is capped at 400 px/s whatever the pad's `launch_speed`. Inspector: group "Launch". **Shatter** (`shatter()`, the contract `crusher.gd` calls): if the subclass's `_shatters_when_crushed()` hook is true (ice; false in the base class and for metal) it runs `_melt()` at once, the block's own melt path rather than a separate delete, so it plays its melt sound, spawns the same per-tile water burst as a full melt, and frees itself; a block that doesn't shatter, or has already melted, ignores it. **Dissolve** (`dissolve()`, called by a `BlockDispenser` on its oldest block): the quiet way out. It does what a melt does to the world (`_leave_world()`: `is_intact()` turns false, physics stops, the block leaves `"water_solid"` and `"ground"`, a hanging player is released, collision is disabled) but spawns no liquid and plays no melt sound; the block is frozen in place, fades its alpha to 0 over `DISSOLVE_TIME` (0.2s) and frees itself. A block already melting or dissolving ignores it. **`spawner_owned`** (plain variable, set before `add_child()`): the block then does not join `"saveable"`, because its dispenser saves and re-creates it (see Block dispenser). Buoyancy helpers: `_submerged_fraction(liquid_type)` (the area of the block's rotated rectangle below the settled liquid surface of the basin at its centre column, a polygon clip, via `WaterManager.get_basin_for_column()` and `get_liquid_surface_world_y()` so crust doesn't count; it also records the submerged area's centroid, the centre of buoyancy) and `_buoyant_accel(submerged, riders, liquid_type)`, `GRAVITY * (1 - submerged / rest_fraction)` (plus, with rotation allowed, the couple between the centre of buoyancy and the centre of mass as angular acceleration) where `rest_fraction = float_depth_fraction + riders * rider_load_tiles / block area` — zero net force at the rest fraction, upward when deeper, and `rest_fraction >= 1.0` can't carry the load and sinks (the carrying-capacity lever for level design). Riders: `_actors_in_band()` (a thin `intersect_shape` band across the top face of the block taken as an upright rectangle, width and height swapped on its side, counting layer-1-only bodies, never a player hanging from this block) is applied the instant a rider is detected, so behaviour doesn't depend on the block having settled. **Rising and draining levels:** a floating block rides the surface. `_damp_vertical_in_liquid(coefficient, submerged, riders, liquid_type, delta)` (used by ice and by metal on slag) damps `velocity.y` relative to the liquid's own vertical speed, `WaterBasin.get_liquid_surface_velocity_y()` scaled by `submerged / rest_fraction` (clamped 0..1), so a block at its rest fraction follows a rising or draining level instead of lagging it, and the surface can't overtake a rider's swim probe. A rider can't hold the block back: in `_integrate_forces()`, while an actor touches the top face (`_rider_on_top`), no roof is hit and every rider has room above (`_riders_free_above`, a `test_move()` per rider in the physics step), a solver-reduced rise (`vel.y > _written_vy` with `_written_vy < 0`) is reset to the written velocity, and the rider is pushed up by its own depenetration. Under a ceiling the contact stops the block as before. The player completes it from its side by leading a rigid floor (see Rising rigid floors under Player). Dropping layer 1 from the block's mask does not work on a rigid body (a pair interacts if either side's mask matches), and a collision exception against the rider makes it fall through (below). **Don't use a collision exception against the rider** — Godot applies exceptions to both bodies, so the rider would fall through. Ledge-hangable via group `"ground"` (`hang_platform` is typed `PhysicsBody2D`, not `AnimatableBody2D`, for this reason; the hang follows the block's whole transform as it bobs, slides or tilts, `_follow_hang_platform()` mapping every pinned point through the platform's change of transform; `is_hangable()` is the block's say: a locked block always, a rotation-allowed one only within `hang_max_tilt_degrees` (10) of resting flat, at most `hang_max_speed` (90 px/s) and `hang_max_spin` (1.2 rad/s), refusing the grab otherwise and force-releasing a held hang (`_force_release_hang()` in `player.gd`, which also stops a mantle in progress); a melt releases a hanging player by clearing `is_hanging`, as `drop_platform.gd` does). While a player's hang is attached to the block (grab through the end of the mantle), the block and player hold a mutual collision exception (`_update_hanger_exception()`), dropped the frame the hang ends: the mantle tween carries the player's collider through the block's top corner, and without the exception the contact between them pushes the block while `_follow_hang_platform()` drags the mantle target with it, so the push can feed itself. **Any other actor-driven teleport onto a `CharacterBody2D` platform needs the same treatment.** Carried by conveyors: `_scan_contacts()` reads `get_conveyor_speed()` off the floor it rests on (ice and metal alike, regardless of slipperiness or weight) as carry, added to the written velocity but never to own motion; a block carried into a wall stops there, with no stored push-back left to creep it away. **Rotation** (`allow_rotation`, default true; false = locked upright): hand-integrated in `_integrate_forces()` like the rest. The solver's angular change (a corner landing, a ledge overhang) is accepted, then a soft spring pulls toward the nearest 90° (`righting_stiffness`) and angular drag (`angular_drag`, plus `angular_drag_liquid` scaled by the submerged fraction) damps it, so a block topples when its support goes but settles flat, upright or on its side. The centre of mass is lowered by `com_drop` (a fraction of half the height). The buoyancy couple lifts a tilted floating block's low side. Riders apply their weight straight down at their feet: a torque `lever_x * weight / gyration²` about the centre of mass (`_rider_torque()`, with `rider_load_tiles` setting how hard), cancelled while the block and its riders together balance over the floor contacts (`RIDER_SUPPORT_TOLERANCE`), so a rider near the edge of a supported block doesn't rock it or the blocks stacked on it, while a rider on the overhang of a perched block still tips it. Actor side contacts are classified in the block's upright frame and the shove guard's angular half only discards spin that increases angular speed beyond what was written. `save_state()` adds `angle` and `angular_velocity` unless the block is locked. Kicks act at the centre of mass (linear velocity only), so a block slides rather than topples. Actors can never shove it: kinematic bodies hand a rigid body velocity through the contact solver, so the shove guard in `_integrate_forces()` (`reject_actor_shove`) removes any horizontal speed an actor's side contact gave it away from that actor, and the displacement it caused; walking into a block, or a block sliding into an actor, just stops it, so it only moves under its own motion. A block it hits is pushed by the contact in proportion to `mass`.
 - **Ice block** (`ice_block.gd`, extends `MeltableBlock`): melts to `WATER` (`melt_time` 2s, `cool_rate` 0.5 — it refreezes); `shunt_speed` 100 px/s, `slide_friction` 70 px/s². Molten slag melts it much faster: while any part of it is below a `SLAG` basin's liquid surface (`_touching_slag()` → `_touching_liquid()`: the four rotated corners and the bottom midpoint, each against its own column's basin; crust doesn't count) progress rises over `slag_melt_time`, so the burst of water lands on the slag and crusts it. Slippery: `is_slippery()` returns true (player floor contract, above), so standing on any ice block gives ice-rink acceleration and friction. `get_surf_friction_scale()` returns `surf_friction_scale` (0.25), so a surfboard coasts about four times as far on it (see Surface friction under Surf). Plays `interactive/ice_shunt` on a kick, `interactive/ice_melt` on melting, `interactive/ice_splash` on first touching water at speed. Buoyant in settled `WATER` only (`float_depth_fraction` 0.8: floats at ~80% submerged, rises back with a small bob if pushed under, lifts off the seabed; slag does not float it), with drag scaled by the submerged fraction — `water_drag` vertically (heavy, so the bob settles; relative to the surface's own vertical speed, see Rising and draining levels in the Meltable block bullet) and the much lighter `water_drag_horizontal` sideways (so a shunt or an underside push keeps drifting until it hits something). The liquid surface used for buoyancy is the basin at the block's centre column, so a wide block half over a wall floats as if over its centre. An actor pressing on the underside of a block that is in water nudges it sideways: `_apply_underside_push()` accelerates the block toward `underside_push_factor` (0.25 by default, 0 = immovable) times the actor's horizontal speed, at a fixed rate, never braking it (water drag stops it), detected with the same kind of band query as riders. This is the only contact-driven horizontal influence an actor has — the block never rides an actor and the shove guard discards sideways contact pushes, so removing this function makes floating blocks immovable by swimming. Art is `sprites/interactive/ice.png`, 16×112 = 7 frames of 16×16 (frame 0 solid, 1–6 the melt), drawn in `_draw_block()` through `MeltableBlock._draw_art()` (see the Meltable block bullet: mirrored tiling for blocks bigger than one tile); `melt_progress` selects the frame (`int(melt_progress * 7)`), so a block that cools and refreezes plays the animation backwards.
 - **Metal block** (`metal_block.gd`, extends `MeltableBlock`): the heavy counterpart to the ice block; melts to `SLAG` (`melt_time` 3s, `cool_rate` 0.4), plays `interactive/metal_melt`. Not slippery. Kickable like ice but sluggish: `shunt_speed` 50 px/s in `metal_block.tscn` (a kick moves it about one tile; `slide_friction` is the shared 70), plays `interactive/metal_shunt`. Also heated by slag: it floats on a `SLAG` basin (`float_depth_fraction` 0.6 in the scene, `rider_load_tiles`, `slag_drag`; only the still-liquid surface counts) but while any of it is below the slag surface at its centre column `melt_progress` rises over `slag_melt_time` (8s), so a slag raft has a limited life. In water it doesn't float: plain gravity slowed by `water_drag`, so it sinks to the seabed. The liquid drag coefficients damp sideways motion too, so a kicked block in liquid stops quickly. Slag onto a water basin crusts it, slag onto a slag basin rejoins it. It jams a crusher: `jams_crusher()` is true while it hasn't melted (see Sawwheel / Crusher hazards). Drawn in code (grey steel glowing orange as it heats) until sprites exist. When its sprite exists, `_draw_block()` should draw it through `_draw_art()` like the ice block so larger blocks get the same mirrored tiling.
 
-Reads with: `liquid-system.md` (melting, buoyancy, crust, slag), `rotating-bodies.md` (block weight and loads on a plank), `hazards-traps.md` (crusher jam and shatter), `lasers-arcs-mirrors.md` (`on_laser_hit()`), `player.md` (kick, rising rigid floors, slippery floors), `player-surf.md` (`surf_shoved()`, the ice friction scale), `platforms-conveyors.md` (conveyors carry blocks).
+- **Block dispenser** (`res://scenes/interactive/block_dispenser.gd` + `block_dispenser.tscn`, `class_name BlockDispenser`, `@tool`): a powered device that makes blocks, so ice and metal are a renewable resource. A plain `Node2D` with a `SpawnPoint` `Marker2D` (default one tile below the node; the block's centre goes there) and a `PowerTarget` child; placeholder art is drawn in code (a steel housing with a light, green = ready, amber = cooling down, and an editor-only outline of the spawn area behind `show_spawn_area`). Exports: `block_scene` (a `PackedScene` whose root is a `MeltableBlock`: `ice_block.tscn` by default, or `metal_block.tscn`), `size_tiles` (applied to each spawned block), `max_alive` (default 1, 0 = unlimited), `cooldown` (default 0.5s, 0 = none), `invert`, `show_spawn_area`. **Power:** a rising edge of the final power state spawns one block; a falling edge does nothing. The startup state is not an edge (`set_powered_initial()` does nothing), so a level never loads with a block waiting, and an unwired dispenser never spawns. **Cooldown:** an edge during the cooldown is dropped silently; the cooldown starts only after a block was actually spawned. **Spawning** runs deferred (a source can change inside a physics callback, where adding a body is not allowed): the block is created upright and at rest as a sibling of the dispenser (so the dispenser's own transform never moves it), fades in over `FADE_IN_TIME` (0.2s) and is solid from its first frame; `interactive/dispenser_spawn` plays at the spawn point. **Occupied spawn area:** before anything else happens, an `intersect_shape` over the block's footprint (inset 0.5px per side, layers 1|2, so the player, enemies, blocks and tiles all count; the same query the door makes before closing) must come back empty. If not, the edge is dropped, `interactive/dispenser_blocked` plays, nothing is retired and no cooldown starts. **`max_alive`:** the dispenser keeps its blocks oldest-first and counts only intact ones, so a melted or shattered block frees its slot. When a new block would exceed the limit, the oldest are retired through `dissolve()` (a quiet fade, no liquid; `interactive/dispenser_dissolve` plays at a lowered volume), and those blocks are left out of the occupancy query, since the block sitting on the spawn point is what is being replaced; the old block is only dissolved if the new one can be made. **Checkpoints:** a spawned block is `spawner_owned`, so it is not saveable; the dispenser is. `Game.apply()` frees every saveable with no snapshot entry and snapshot keys are node paths, so a runtime block could never survive a restore on its own. Each block gets the explicit name `<dispenser name>_block_<serial>` (the serial is saved and never reused). `save_state()` returns each live block's name and its own `save_state()` (so exactly what `MeltableBlock` saves), the serial and the cooldown left; a dissolving block is not saved. `load_state()` frees any block the fresh copy already has, re-creates the saved ones (no sound, no fade, no edge, independent of restore order) and calls each one's `load_state()`; a restore never spawns. A level that is restored therefore has the blocks that were alive at the checkpoint and none made since. Level design: the spawn area must be free of anything solid when the edge arrives, the dispenser dissolves its own oldest block but never another dispenser's, and a block it spawned is an ordinary block (kickable, meltable, a plate weight, a hang) until it goes. The dispenser is a general mechanism for runtime-created saveables: the owner names them, they stay out of `"saveable"`, and the owner saves and re-creates them.
+
+Reads with: `liquid-system.md` (melting, buoyancy, crust, slag), `rotating-bodies.md` (block weight and loads on a plank), `hazards-traps.md` (crusher jam and shatter), `lasers-arcs-mirrors.md` (`on_laser_hit()`), `player.md` (kick, rising rigid floors, slippery floors), `player-surf.md` (`surf_shoved()`, the ice friction scale), `platforms-conveyors.md` (conveyors carry blocks), `power.md` (the dispenser is a powered device), `saving-levels-ui.md` (the saveable contract the dispenser satisfies for its blocks).
